WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:11.700
AMR AWADALLAH: Hi, I'm Amr.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:15.150
I lead developer
relations at Google Cloud.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:17.190
What's developer
relations, you might ask.

00:00:17.190 --> 00:00:19.920
We are engineers
who love talking

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:22.410
to people who are hands-on.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.040
That includes developers,
operators, data engineers,

00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:29.430
data scientists, any
technical practitioner

00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:33.870
who makes the Information
Technology world go round.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:39.510
We are constantly inspired
by and in awe of what you do.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.630
We succeed by supporting
you along the journey

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:47.040
to modernize your
applications and innovate.

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.780
Ever since Google Cloud
started working with you,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.810
we learned a lot
about the challenges

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.930
of building and operating
enterprise workloads

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:57.300
while also dealing with
the business imperative

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.610
to constantly innovate.

00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:01.980
For instance, we
see teams struggling

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.650
with these four problem areas.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:06.930
First, they are
constantly occupied

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:09.570
with lots of non-productive,
repetitive tasks,

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:12.300
and in many cases, reinventing
the wheel for things

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.410
that have been figured
out many times over.

00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:19.370
Second, the businesses are
afraid of frequently updating

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.010
their high-value workloads
due to stability and security

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:26.330
concerns, which significantly
hinders the rate of innovation.

00:01:26.330 --> 00:01:29.120
Third, they struggle to keep
up with all the mandates

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:32.450
around compliance, auditing,
security, availability,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.240
you name it.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.270
And fourth, it's
almost impossible

00:01:37.270 --> 00:01:40.480
to write applications once,
then have them run anywhere,

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:44.170
whether that be on-premises,
in the cloud, or at Edge.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.000
And as you know,
this isn't about

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:47.618
whether you're in
the cloud or not.

00:01:47.618 --> 00:01:49.660
Many of you have made the
transition to the cloud

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.240
already, but you still
face these challenges.

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:55.910
We want to help you
significantly accelerate

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:59.510
the speed with which you
solve, create, and innovate.

00:01:59.510 --> 00:02:02.090
We do this in three ways.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:05.210
First, we think it's
important to automate

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:06.930
as much as possible.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:09.380
This means eliminating
repeatable tasks

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:12.800
in pursuit of important
objectives such as secure

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.250
by default,
monitoring by default,

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:18.900
canary testing, and continuous
integration and delivery.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:22.640
In other words, let's stop
reinventing the wheel.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.630
Second, we want to
increase your productivity.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:29.570
We know that everyone comes
from different backgrounds

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.100
and works in
different environments

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:32.420
with different tools.

00:02:32.420 --> 00:02:34.730
We embrace this
diversity by supporting

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:37.520
your favorite open-source
frameworks and enterprise

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.020
software applications.

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.600
And we do this, as
you'll see in this talk,

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.850
not just on the infrastructure
side with efforts

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:46.580
like Kubernetes, but
also for developers

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.850
by supporting code editors
like IntelliJ and Visual Studio

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.560
Code.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.350
Third, you need
portability options

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.480
for where your workloads run.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.140
Our goal is to enable you to
write your applications once,

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.510
then have them run
anywhere with minimal

00:03:02.510 --> 00:03:05.450
to no code changes while
transparently carrying

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.030
over all the
security, governance,

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.670
and observability requirements.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.070
To demonstrate how
Google Cloud helps you

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:15.380
with all of this,
my colleagues and I

00:03:15.380 --> 00:03:17.330
will highlight the
key parts of a journey

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:20.750
from migration to innovation.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.910
We will use a sample
financial application

00:03:22.910 --> 00:03:26.930
that we call Demo Bank, a
traditional two-tier business

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.030
application that
simulates a bank.

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.130
It's already been migrated
from virtual machines

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.460
to containers running
on Google Cloud.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:38.470
What's next in the modernization
journey for Demo Bank?

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:39.940
Let's talk to my
colleague, Megan,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:43.420
the Developer Relations Engineer
for Anthos, to find out.

00:03:43.420 --> 00:03:44.140
Hi, Megan.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:45.140
Great to see you.

00:03:45.140 --> 00:03:46.750
MEGAN O'KEEFE: Hi, Amr.

00:03:46.750 --> 00:03:49.600
AMR AWADALLAH: I heard you run
on coffee, so before you start,

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:51.187
would you like some?

00:03:51.187 --> 00:03:52.270
MEGAN O'KEEFE: Absolutely.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:53.395
AMR AWADALLAH: Here you go.

00:03:53.395 --> 00:03:54.940
[VORTEX]

00:03:54.940 --> 00:03:57.115
MEGAN O'KEEFE: Thank you.

00:03:57.115 --> 00:03:58.990
Let's take a look at
how Demo Bank is running

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:01.240
in containers on Anthos.

00:04:01.240 --> 00:04:03.130
I'm starting in the
Google Cloud Console,

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:06.460
where I've deployed the app onto
a Kubernetes Engine cluster.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:08.800
In my project, I actually
have multiple clusters

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:11.830
running for development,
staging, and production,

00:04:11.830 --> 00:04:14.680
plus a cluster running in
AWS, which I've registered

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:16.870
to the Anthos dashboard.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.600
This AWS cluster is running
some legacy workloads

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:21.490
that we're trying to
migrate into Google,

00:04:21.490 --> 00:04:23.890
but for now we can see
the status of that cluster

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:25.428
in the Anthos dashboard.

00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:27.220
Let's take a look at
the production cluster

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:28.550
to see what's running.

00:04:28.550 --> 00:04:31.670
Demo Bank is a two-tier web
app with a frontend and two

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:32.700
backends.

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.760
One backend is written in
Python, the other in Java.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:38.470
The backends are monolithic,
each with an embedded SQL

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:39.850
database.

00:04:39.850 --> 00:04:42.520
As someone who works with
platform administrators a lot,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.380
something I really
like about Anthos

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:47.330
is the observability you
get right out of the box.

00:04:47.330 --> 00:04:50.380
Let's navigate to Anthos
Service Mesh to check it out.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.620
When I migrated Demo Bank from
virtual machines to containers

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:57.020
running on Anthos, each
workload here-- the frontend,

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.210
the account service--

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.240
has Anthos Service Mesh enabled.

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:02.470
A service mesh tracks
all the requests

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:05.110
flowing through the app
and generates metrics

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:07.840
like requests
throughput and latency.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.870
I didn't have to refactor the
workloads to get these metrics.

00:05:10.870 --> 00:05:12.280
It all just works.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.520
And the result is that I can see
a live network topology diagram

00:05:15.520 --> 00:05:17.510
of the application at runtime.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:19.660
So as an admin or a
platform engineer,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:21.850
the Anthos dashboard
is like my co-pilot,

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:23.920
showing me what's
happening with the app.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.280
From this dashboard, I can also
dial into one of these services

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:29.050
to view metrics
and service health.

00:05:29.050 --> 00:05:32.620
From there, I can set up service
level objectives, or SLOs,

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:34.780
to ensure that the app
is meeting any required

00:05:34.780 --> 00:05:38.260
thresholds for availability
and performance.

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:40.960
Anthos Service Mesh is also
integrated into Google Cloud

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.600
Monitoring, so I can
create a single dashboard

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:46.240
to see all the metrics
relevant to the application,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.850
from the app code level,
to database metrics,

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:51.290
all the way down to the
underlying infrastructure.

00:05:51.290 --> 00:05:54.550
And because I just migrated
this app from VMs to containers,

00:05:54.550 --> 00:05:56.200
these dashboards
are really useful

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:58.930
and will help me figure out
how to right-size my workloads

00:05:58.930 --> 00:06:00.910
and set appropriate
resource requests.

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.940
Overall, moving existing
workloads into Anthos

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.490
helps me gain deep
visibility into this app

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:08.530
at all levels of the stack.

00:06:08.530 --> 00:06:09.572
Catch you later, Amr.

00:06:09.572 --> 00:06:10.780
AMR AWADALLAH: Thanks, Megan.

00:06:10.780 --> 00:06:12.780
This looks great.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:16.620
However, this is not the end
of the modernization journey.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:18.623
Modernization is a
continuous journey

00:06:18.623 --> 00:06:20.040
to improve your
ability to deliver

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:25.740
a quality solution easier,
faster, and more securely.

00:06:25.740 --> 00:06:28.170
After the migration
to Anthos, Demo Bank

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:30.840
is now more secure,
available, and responsive.

00:06:30.840 --> 00:06:32.730
But next, they
need to accelerate

00:06:32.730 --> 00:06:35.160
the rate with which they
deliver improvements

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:38.160
and innovations to meet the
ever-increasing customer

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.350
expectations.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.840
In their case, Demo
Bank wants to expand

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:47.160
its online capabilities and
serve more lines of business.

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:50.100
To do this, we can adopt a
microservices architecture

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.460
versus two-tier so that we can
scale out just the parts that

00:06:53.460 --> 00:06:56.670
need it, and featured teams
can own individual services

00:06:56.670 --> 00:06:59.850
to release more
quickly and frequently.

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:02.010
To help with this
modernization journey,

00:07:02.010 --> 00:07:05.140
Google Cloud provides
end-to-end development tooling

00:07:05.140 --> 00:07:07.710
which allows you to focus
on your business logic,

00:07:07.710 --> 00:07:11.430
and automation takes care of
filling in the rest for you.

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.800
Furthermore, you can
iterate more quickly

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:15.820
with the confidence of
knowing that automated

00:07:15.820 --> 00:07:18.510
observability will detect
production issues early

00:07:18.510 --> 00:07:20.070
for you.

00:07:20.070 --> 00:07:22.170
Let's now talk to Daniel,
a Software Engineer

00:07:22.170 --> 00:07:25.320
working on Spring Boot
integrations for Google Cloud,

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.150
and see how we can
quickly develop

00:07:27.150 --> 00:07:30.120
a new microservice using
tools that you are already

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:30.780
familiar with.

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:32.624
DANIEL ZOU: (QUIETLY)
Thank you, Amr.

00:07:32.624 --> 00:07:34.470
So to modernize--

00:07:34.470 --> 00:07:36.690
AMR AWADALLAH: Hey,
Daniel, we can't hear you.

00:07:36.690 --> 00:07:38.260
Try this mic.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:39.580
DANIEL ZOU: Oh.

00:07:39.580 --> 00:07:42.160
Thank you, Amr.

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.380
So to modernize our
application, let's try

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.140
refactoring the authentication
features of Demo Bank

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.970
to a separate microservice.

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:51.550
This area of our app has
experienced a lot of growth

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:53.170
and increased traffic.

00:07:53.170 --> 00:07:54.950
Normally, this task
would be complex,

00:07:54.950 --> 00:07:56.325
but we're going
to take advantage

00:07:56.325 --> 00:07:58.570
of a variety of Google
Cloud-managed services

00:07:58.570 --> 00:08:00.670
for less maintenance
and better availability,

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:04.570
and also use various tools to
make use of Cloud much easier.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:05.950
For the new
microservice, we will

00:08:05.950 --> 00:08:08.920
use Cloud SQL for our
database, and we'll

00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:10.570
configure it for
high availability

00:08:10.570 --> 00:08:12.550
with the click of a button.

00:08:12.550 --> 00:08:15.340
Next, let's start
writing some code.

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:18.160
Our development team uses Java
and prefers the Spring Boot

00:08:18.160 --> 00:08:19.030
framework.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:22.300
We'll create a Spring Boot
service from start.spring.io

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.090
to generate some starter
code for this project.

00:08:25.090 --> 00:08:27.500
Select GCP Support
as a dependency,

00:08:27.500 --> 00:08:29.050
which allows us to
easily integrate

00:08:29.050 --> 00:08:30.175
with Google Cloud services.

00:08:30.175 --> 00:08:32.980
Now as we write code,
we're able to just focus

00:08:32.980 --> 00:08:34.142
on the business logic.

00:08:34.142 --> 00:08:35.559
There will be some
points where we

00:08:35.559 --> 00:08:38.980
can leverage more cloud services
in addition to the database.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:40.390
For example, we
use a private key

00:08:40.390 --> 00:08:42.730
to encrypt
authentication tokens.

00:08:42.730 --> 00:08:45.640
Previously, this key was stored
in an on-premises secret store

00:08:45.640 --> 00:08:48.820
like HashiCorp Vault.
On Google Cloud,

00:08:48.820 --> 00:08:51.770
you can continue to use Vault if
you need to, but for this demo,

00:08:51.770 --> 00:08:53.770
we'll switch to Google
Secrets Manager, which

00:08:53.770 --> 00:08:56.050
is a secure way of storing
secrets on the cloud

00:08:56.050 --> 00:08:58.880
so you don't have to manage
the infrastructure yourself.

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.140
We can use Spring Cloud GCP
libraries to access the secret

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.330
through the application's
configuration

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:05.960
without any extra code.

00:09:05.960 --> 00:09:08.860
Spring Cloud GCP is one of
the many libraries and tools

00:09:08.860 --> 00:09:10.810
maintained by Google
in partnership

00:09:10.810 --> 00:09:12.700
with the open-source community.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:14.920
Also, as part of the
modernization effort,

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:18.070
we'll trace service calls,
have centralized logging,

00:09:18.070 --> 00:09:20.380
and generate custom metrics.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:23.350
Using Spring Cloud GCP,
all of these features

00:09:23.350 --> 00:09:27.230
can be added to our app
through configuration alone.

00:09:27.230 --> 00:09:28.600
So this is the big idea.

00:09:28.600 --> 00:09:31.740
We don't have to write any
extra code to do all this.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:34.060
And with minimal
configuration, you can now

00:09:34.060 --> 00:09:37.090
see in-application
traces in Cloud Trace,

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.970
and clicking Show Logs will
show you the logs associated

00:09:39.970 --> 00:09:41.570
with each request.

00:09:41.570 --> 00:09:44.450
You can also see JVM and
application-level metrics

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:47.990
exported to Cloud Monitoring
and explore them in a dashboard.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:50.110
Now because our application
has no custom code

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:52.360
to access Google
Cloud Services, it's

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:55.600
portable across
hybrid environments.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:09:58.750
If you need to deploy the
same application on-premises,

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:02.170
simply change the configuration
without modifying the code.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.540
And similarly, an
on-premises application

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:06.850
can easily adopt
Google Cloud Services

00:10:06.850 --> 00:10:08.590
through just configuration.

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:10.720
Let's test everything
before we shift it.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.630
For Cloud SQL, to start the
post-stress SQL instance

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.430
with data for
testing, we can use

00:10:15.430 --> 00:10:18.700
a third-party open-source
project called Test Containers.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:20.920
Now the database
container is automatically

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.260
started when we run
an integration test.

00:10:23.260 --> 00:10:26.350
For cloud databases such as
Firestore or Cloud Spanner,

00:10:26.350 --> 00:10:28.510
you can use our local emulators.

00:10:28.510 --> 00:10:31.060
We'll use a tool called Jib
to containerize our Java

00:10:31.060 --> 00:10:32.360
application.

00:10:32.360 --> 00:10:34.120
Another alternative
is a buildpack which

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:36.070
can achieve a similar outcome.

00:10:36.070 --> 00:10:38.260
These tools let you
use best practices

00:10:38.260 --> 00:10:40.990
without needing deep
expertise in container tools.

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:44.150
Let's also run our application
locally to test it out.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:46.420
I can easily do this with
the Cloud Code extension

00:10:46.420 --> 00:10:49.000
in either IntelliJ
or VS Code IDE

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:51.040
and launch the entire
stack in Minikube

00:10:51.040 --> 00:10:54.700
so I can see everything working
end-to-end on my local machine.

00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:56.560
If I make a change
to the code now,

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:59.110
Cloud Code will automatically
redeploy my changes

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:00.460
into the local environment.

00:11:00.460 --> 00:11:02.230
All right, everything
looks good.

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:03.980
I'm ready to check in
the code and see how

00:11:03.980 --> 00:11:05.920
it gets pushed to production.

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.010
Back to you, Amr.

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:08.260
AMR AWADALLAH: Thanks, Daniel.

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:09.790
That was awesome.

00:11:09.790 --> 00:11:11.830
As Daniel showed
you, Google Cloud

00:11:11.830 --> 00:11:14.200
provides comprehensive
end-to-end development

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:17.800
tooling, namely, IDE
extensions, integrations

00:11:17.800 --> 00:11:22.060
with widely used frameworks, and
fully managed cloud services.

00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:25.160
This significantly
accelerates your productivity

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:27.340
so you can realize the true
potential of application

00:11:27.340 --> 00:11:30.870
modernization while using skills
and tools you already know

00:11:30.870 --> 00:11:32.230
and have.

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:33.700
So what's next?

00:11:33.700 --> 00:11:36.220
It looks like we're ready to
deploy Daniel's new service

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:38.440
into our Anthos environment.

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:41.380
Google Cloud is here
to help you with that.

00:11:41.380 --> 00:11:44.090
As we increase the number
of Demo Bank services,

00:11:44.090 --> 00:11:46.960
it's important that we build
consistency and automation

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:48.950
in our processes.

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:51.520
This is where our continuous
integration and deployment

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:54.570
commonly known as
CI/CD comes in.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:11:59.700
CI/CD helps you to quickly,
confidently, and safely

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:04.180
roll out updates for
many services in peril.

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:09.980
This means you can innovate with
lower risk and higher velocity.

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:11.530
Let's bring Megan
back to show you

00:12:11.530 --> 00:12:14.560
how Google Cloud supports
you to release new services

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.950
in a secure and low-risk way.

00:12:17.950 --> 00:12:19.120
Hi, Megan.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.386
Before you start,
have some more coffee.

00:12:21.386 --> 00:12:22.140
MEGAN O'KEEFE: Ah.

00:12:22.140 --> 00:12:23.980
Thank you, Amr.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:25.900
Now let's take a look
at Daniel's pull request

00:12:25.900 --> 00:12:27.960
in the Demo Bank repository.

00:12:27.960 --> 00:12:31.510
The repo uses GitHub Actions
for continuous integration.

00:12:31.510 --> 00:12:34.480
Here on Daniel's branch, we
have integration and functional

00:12:34.480 --> 00:12:37.480
tests running to make sure
that Daniel's code changes are

00:12:37.480 --> 00:12:40.690
compatible with the
other Demo Bank APIs.

00:12:40.690 --> 00:12:44.080
Once I review Daniel's code,
I can go ahead and hit Merge.

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.290
Doing this will automatically
trigger a release pipeline

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:48.940
running in Google Cloud Build.

00:12:48.940 --> 00:12:51.700
This pipeline builds the
Demo Bank container images,

00:12:51.700 --> 00:12:55.640
then uses Binary Authorization
to sign all the images.

00:12:55.640 --> 00:12:59.110
This helps us ensure that any
container image deployed to GKE

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:00.490
is safe and trusted.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.490
Once this pipeline completes,
another Cloud Build pipeline

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:04.180
starts up.

00:13:04.180 --> 00:13:06.370
This is our continuous
deployment pipeline,

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.590
which takes the release
deployment manifests,

00:13:08.590 --> 00:13:10.570
configured with
the new image tags,

00:13:10.570 --> 00:13:13.270
and applies it to the
production GKE cluster.

00:13:13.270 --> 00:13:15.160
Then from our development
Slack channel,

00:13:15.160 --> 00:13:16.810
Cloud Build sends
us a notification

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:19.300
that the new service has
successfully deployed.

00:13:19.300 --> 00:13:20.920
Now that the
container is running,

00:13:20.920 --> 00:13:24.010
I can control how traffic
gets sent to this new service

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:25.810
to ensure a safe roll-out.

00:13:25.810 --> 00:13:28.030
Because the existing
account service is still

00:13:28.030 --> 00:13:30.040
serving requests,
I'm going to keep it

00:13:30.040 --> 00:13:32.180
running in the cluster for now.

00:13:32.180 --> 00:13:36.130
But I can use Service Mesh
to rewrite HTTP requests

00:13:36.130 --> 00:13:38.260
from the account
service monolith

00:13:38.260 --> 00:13:41.440
to the new user service
with no code changes

00:13:41.440 --> 00:13:43.840
and, on top of that, send
only a small percentage

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:46.180
of that traffic to
Daniel's user service.

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:49.030
Then I can return to the
Anthos Service Mesh dashboard

00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:51.310
to view metrics for how
the user service is faring

00:13:51.310 --> 00:13:53.890
in production, then
incrementally send more traffic

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.960
to it once I know it's healthy.

00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:57.670
I can even take
it a step further

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.730
and configure security
policies for this new service.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:02.740
Anthos Config
Management is a tool

00:14:02.740 --> 00:14:05.740
that allows you to push
policy configuration to a Git

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:09.130
repository and automatically
sync those policies down

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:10.930
to your clusters.

00:14:10.930 --> 00:14:13.180
This can help you
apply org-wide policies

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:15.760
across many services at once.

00:14:15.760 --> 00:14:18.160
Because we're dealing with
a banking application,

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:20.900
data security is
really important.

00:14:20.900 --> 00:14:23.260
So one thing I can
do is create a policy

00:14:23.260 --> 00:14:26.740
to encrypt all HTTP traffic
across all the Demo Bank

00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:27.820
workloads.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.120
Once Anthos Config Management
syncs this policy from Git,

00:14:31.120 --> 00:14:33.100
I can return to the
Service Mesh dashboard

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:35.820
to see the encryption status
for all of our services.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:39.250
Here I can now see that traffic
to Daniel's new user service

00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:42.200
is encrypted with mutual
TLS authentication.

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:44.800
So that's how I use Google
Cloud to put a new service

00:14:44.800 --> 00:14:46.990
into production on Anthos.

00:14:46.990 --> 00:14:48.577
Over to you, Amr.

00:14:48.577 --> 00:14:50.410
AMR AWADALLAH: Thanks,
Megan, for showing us

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:54.430
the CI/CD process for
Demo Bank services.

00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:57.890
I think this is super
cool for several reasons.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:02.140
First, it's really compelling
how safety and risk reduction

00:15:02.140 --> 00:15:06.250
are top of mind throughout
the entire release process.

00:15:06.250 --> 00:15:09.180
We ensure the safety of
the deployment itself,

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.950
its ability to serve
traffic without errors,

00:15:11.950 --> 00:15:14.740
and the authenticity
of the release artifact

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:17.740
through Binary Authorization.

00:15:17.740 --> 00:15:20.410
Second, notice how the
automation supported

00:15:20.410 --> 00:15:23.830
by cloud-builds, GitHub
Actions, and Anthos policies

00:15:23.830 --> 00:15:26.980
help build that safety.

00:15:26.980 --> 00:15:31.210
Automation reduces human
input, which, in turn, reduces

00:15:31.210 --> 00:15:33.650
the possibility of human error.

00:15:33.650 --> 00:15:36.790
This allows us to deploy new
code changes several times

00:15:36.790 --> 00:15:40.930
per day, confident that, for
instance, user data encryption

00:15:40.930 --> 00:15:44.290
will automatically
be maintained.

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:47.110
A very key point
of this entire talk

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:50.710
is that safety speeds up
innovation and increases

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.000
room for creativity.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:55.420
Now that we're here, we
are in a good position

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:59.320
to take advantage of more
of what Google Cloud offers.

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.810
In this case, let's see
what machine learning

00:16:01.810 --> 00:16:05.110
can do for our financial
services workloads.

00:16:05.110 --> 00:16:07.548
Let's call Sara.

00:16:07.548 --> 00:16:08.590
SARA ROBINSON: High, Amr.

00:16:08.590 --> 00:16:11.230
Having our application
and data in Google Cloud

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:14.680
allows us to easily tap into
other parts of our platform.

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:17.740
In our app, finding a way to
flag fraudulent transactions

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.800
quickly will save everyone
a lot of time and money.

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:23.170
Machine learning is
a great fit for this.

00:16:23.170 --> 00:16:26.260
I'll use BigQuery, AutoML
Tables, and AI Platform

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.570
Notebooks to build
our solution, and I'll

00:16:28.570 --> 00:16:30.850
use the explainability
tools in AutoML

00:16:30.850 --> 00:16:34.000
to understand how our model
is making predictions.

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:37.240
As a data scientist, I'd like
to do my data pre-processing

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:39.820
from a notebook environment
where I can interact directly

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:41.410
with BigQuery data.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:43.810
I'll do this with Cloud
AI Platform Notebooks.

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:45.820
We're using a publicly
available BigQuery data

00:16:45.820 --> 00:16:48.730
set of credit card transactions
to train our model.

00:16:48.730 --> 00:16:52.150
For each transaction, we have
data on the time, amount,

00:16:52.150 --> 00:16:53.870
and a few other variables.

00:16:53.870 --> 00:16:56.440
We see that the original data
set is heavily imbalanced,

00:16:56.440 --> 00:16:59.020
which could negatively affect
the quality of our model.

00:16:59.020 --> 00:17:01.150
To reduce the imbalance,
I'm using a technique

00:17:01.150 --> 00:17:03.790
called downsampling to
take all of the examples

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:07.150
from our minority class,
fraud, and a random sample

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:09.040
of our majority
class, while still

00:17:09.040 --> 00:17:12.640
maintaining a diverse sample
of the non-fraud data.

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:14.819
Next, I am saving
the updated data

00:17:14.819 --> 00:17:17.290
set to a new BigQuery table.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:20.200
This data is now ready
to be sent to a model.

00:17:20.200 --> 00:17:24.260
To build, train, and deploy our
model, I'll use AutoML Tables.

00:17:24.260 --> 00:17:25.900
It will let me do
all of these things

00:17:25.900 --> 00:17:28.060
without writing any model code.

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:30.850
With AutoML Tables, I
can train an ML model

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:32.980
while keeping my
data in BigQuery.

00:17:32.980 --> 00:17:35.380
When I press Train,
AutoML takes care

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:37.960
of transforming our data
into the correct format

00:17:37.960 --> 00:17:40.240
and finding the best
model type for our tasks.

00:17:40.240 --> 00:17:43.060
Fortunately, AutoML has
some optimization methods

00:17:43.060 --> 00:17:45.940
to handle the remaining data
imbalance after downsampling.

00:17:45.940 --> 00:17:48.160
Here I'm choosing to optimize
our model's performance

00:17:48.160 --> 00:17:50.460
on our less common
class during training.

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:52.960
Now that our model is trained,
let's look at some evaluation

00:17:52.960 --> 00:17:53.530
metrics.

00:17:53.530 --> 00:17:55.870
The confusion matrix
tells us the percentage

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.450
from each class that our
model predicted correctly.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:01.900
This model reached 86% accuracy
with no false positives

00:18:01.900 --> 00:18:04.210
on our fraud class, which
is looking pretty good.

00:18:04.210 --> 00:18:07.090
Rather than writing our own
algorithm of hard-coded rules

00:18:07.090 --> 00:18:09.310
for catching fraudulent
transactions,

00:18:09.310 --> 00:18:12.610
we built a model to do this by
finding patterns in our data.

00:18:12.610 --> 00:18:14.410
Even with a
high-quality model, it's

00:18:14.410 --> 00:18:18.100
important to understand what
led our model to predict fraud.

00:18:18.100 --> 00:18:21.550
Through the Explainable AI
integration with AutoML Tables,

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:23.530
we can see which features
our model is relying

00:18:23.530 --> 00:18:25.280
on most to make a prediction.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:28.220
I can now deploy my
model with one click,

00:18:28.220 --> 00:18:30.010
then I can get
predictions in a UI

00:18:30.010 --> 00:18:32.380
or with the AutoML
Prediction API.

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:33.100
Let's try it out.

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:34.573
[MYSTERIOUS MUSIC PLAYING]

00:18:34.573 --> 00:18:36.490
We'll make a transaction
with this credit card

00:18:36.490 --> 00:18:38.980
and see if our model
flags it as fraud.

00:18:38.980 --> 00:18:41.020
Looks like this is not
a real credit card.

00:18:41.020 --> 00:18:42.680
Yikes.

00:18:42.680 --> 00:18:43.300
Oh, wait.

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:46.780
This is a banking demo,
not a baking demo.

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:49.900
Let's go back to the code.

00:18:49.900 --> 00:18:52.900
I'm making a request to the
AutoML Tables Prediction API

00:18:52.900 --> 00:18:55.780
for my notebook to get a
test prediction on our model

00:18:55.780 --> 00:18:58.050
with feature attributions.

00:18:58.050 --> 00:19:00.880
In the API response, we
get the model's prediction

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:02.350
and the instance-level
attribution

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.410
values indicating which features
our model relied on most

00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:06.870
to make this prediction.

00:19:06.870 --> 00:19:08.770
From my notebook's
instance, I can

00:19:08.770 --> 00:19:11.470
create a new version of the data
set and even programmatically

00:19:11.470 --> 00:19:13.450
kick off model retraining.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.070
Let's train our
model for more time

00:19:15.070 --> 00:19:18.110
and see if AutoML is able
to create a better model.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:20.860
In addition to testing our
deployed model from a notebook,

00:19:20.860 --> 00:19:23.087
we also want to get
predictions from our web app.

00:19:23.087 --> 00:19:24.670
We want to keep the
client code simple

00:19:24.670 --> 00:19:26.962
and handle calling the model
and parsing the prediction

00:19:26.962 --> 00:19:28.798
response on the server.

00:19:28.798 --> 00:19:31.090
I'll take the Python code
that calls our deployed model

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:32.920
and put it in Cloud Run.

00:19:32.920 --> 00:19:35.260
Cloud Run lets me take
code in any language

00:19:35.260 --> 00:19:37.930
and deploy it in a scalable
container environment

00:19:37.930 --> 00:19:40.960
without worrying about
infrastructure management.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.880
With two commands, I can deploy
the container to Cloud Run

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.880
so that it's triggered
whenever a transaction is

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.340
made from our banking app.

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:50.560
As we iterate on our
Python code and model,

00:19:50.560 --> 00:19:53.710
we can also make use of Cloud
Run's traffic splitting feature

00:19:53.710 --> 00:19:55.300
to serve new versions
of our service

00:19:55.300 --> 00:19:57.880
to a small percentage of users.

00:19:57.880 --> 00:19:59.680
To test out my
Cloud Run service,

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:02.270
I'm making a curl
request to it here.

00:20:02.270 --> 00:20:04.038
Although the
service is deployed,

00:20:04.038 --> 00:20:06.580
I'm only charged for the time
it's being used since Cloud Run

00:20:06.580 --> 00:20:08.990
supports auto-scaling
by default.

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:11.800
This means it'll scale to zero
when there are no requests.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:14.740
And just like that, from data
pre-processing in BigQuery

00:20:14.740 --> 00:20:17.040
to a deployed
AutoML Tables model,

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:20.830
we built an end-to-end
fraud detection solution.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:23.320
With the right tools,
machine learning really

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:24.850
is a piece of cake.

00:20:24.850 --> 00:20:26.953
Would you like some, Amr?

00:20:26.953 --> 00:20:28.120
AMR AWADALLAH: Thanks, Sara.

00:20:28.120 --> 00:20:29.780
That cake looks delicious.

00:20:29.780 --> 00:20:31.940
I can't wait to taste it.

00:20:31.940 --> 00:20:34.340
That was very impressive.

00:20:34.340 --> 00:20:36.960
We just saw how Sara
applied fraud detection

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:38.890
to our financial
services scenario

00:20:38.890 --> 00:20:41.830
using tools she already knows.

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:44.680
I believe that every industry
could innovate around

00:20:44.680 --> 00:20:46.900
the kind of cloud
scale data analytics

00:20:46.900 --> 00:20:49.510
and machine learning made
easily available to your data

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:53.380
engineers, data
scientists, and analysts.

00:20:53.380 --> 00:20:56.590
Also, stepping back, as
Megan and Daniel showed you,

00:20:56.590 --> 00:20:59.250
we are bringing to dev
and ops an integrated

00:20:59.250 --> 00:21:03.060
set of automated capabilities
that represent the best of what

00:21:03.060 --> 00:21:05.710
Google has learned
about code, workloads,

00:21:05.710 --> 00:21:08.340
and tooling over many
years while developing

00:21:08.340 --> 00:21:11.940
our own planet-scale
consumer services.

00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:16.020
The end result is that we make
you as productive as possible

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:18.420
while improving the
quality of your software

00:21:18.420 --> 00:21:23.340
and enabling faster and
safer release rates.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:25.620
We bring this to you through
your favorite open-source

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:29.070
on third-party frameworks,
enterprise applications, tools,

00:21:29.070 --> 00:21:32.033
and languages, meeting
you where you are,

00:21:32.033 --> 00:21:34.200
so you can start solving
immediately with the skills

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:35.700
and code you really have.

00:21:35.700 --> 00:21:38.370
You saw Megan use
Anthos and Cloud Build

00:21:38.370 --> 00:21:40.890
to deploy and operate
containerized applications

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.970
in a safe, predictable way.

00:21:42.970 --> 00:21:44.520
This means developers
can focus more

00:21:44.520 --> 00:21:47.380
on the application logic
versus the infrastructure,

00:21:47.380 --> 00:21:51.160
allowing them to iterate
at a faster rate.

00:21:51.160 --> 00:21:54.670
You saw Daniel use Spring
Boot and Spring Cloud GCP

00:21:54.670 --> 00:21:57.240
to set up tracing,
logging, and monitoring

00:21:57.240 --> 00:21:59.730
with only configuration changes.

00:21:59.730 --> 00:22:01.710
This also means
that your code is

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:06.000
portable to any cloud provider
on-premises or the Edge.

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.280
And you saw Sara
go from raw data

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.860
to a trained,
deployed AutoML model

00:22:10.860 --> 00:22:13.320
all without moving
data from BigQuery.

00:22:13.320 --> 00:22:16.410
This means you can train
high-quality models with less

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.740
code, allowing you to focus
on improving the predictions

00:22:19.740 --> 00:22:22.980
and getting them faster
to your end users.

00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:26.250
To learn more, we have the
Google Cloud Certification

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:29.040
Program, which allows you to
specialize in our products

00:22:29.040 --> 00:22:31.290
